# The adic valuation of a place is trivial on the base field

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.Place.adicValuation_isTrivialOn`
(`5ca17c5e-d2aa-5bb0-ad8c-7d8e397d95c6`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `7d141da6-77db-5781-be8b-2fb2cd60d7ba`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the
sense of the structure [`AlgebraicCurve.Place`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22): a
valuation subring $\mathcal{O}_v \subseteq F$ which contains $\mathrm{algebraMap}\,K\,F(a)$ for
every $a \in K$, which is not the whole of $F$, and whose ideals are all principal (so that
$\mathcal{O}_v$ is a discrete valuation ring). Attached to $v$ is its height one prime
[`AlgebraicCurve.Place.heightOneSpectrum`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L95), namely
the maximal ideal of $\mathcal{O}_v$ viewed as a point of the height one spectrum, and the
associated $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation
[`AlgebraicCurve.Place.adicValuation`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L102) on the
fraction field $F$, i.e. the normalised valuation whose value group is $\mathbb{Z}$ with a zero
adjoined. The theorem asserts that this valuation satisfies `Valuation.IsTrivialOn K`, that is, it
is trivial on the image of the base field $K$ in $F$: no element of $K$ has a zero or a pole at $v$.

This is the standard fact, in the language of algebraic function fields, that a place of $F/K$ is a
place over $K$, so that the base field consists of $v$-units; it records the triviality of $v$ on
$K$ in a form usable as an instance or hypothesis elsewhere. It is used in the analysis of valuation
subrings of a rational function field, via
(`AlgebraicCurve.RationalFunctionField.toValuationSubring_eq_of_forall_ne_ofHeightOneSpectrum`).

